nodecode-sound-effects is a rewrite of NousResearch's NousResearch/hermes-official-plugins:

  https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-official-plugins
  commit e7a218f26db88426ab3e7376f0e000b6e356f5e7
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Nous Research (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the elevenlabs_sound_effect tool and its whole call path -- the
prompt/duration/loop/influence/model arguments, the refusal of an oversize
prompt, of a length written in the prompt instead of in duration_seconds, and
of a length outside 0.5 to 30 seconds; the per-profile settings for the model,
the audio format, the API base URL and the key (an inline secret or the
environment variable it names); the streaming POST to
/v1/sound-generation?output_format=... under a 50 MB cap and a 120-second
timeout; the check that the bytes really are the container that was asked for;
the saved, never-overwritten file and the honest playing time read out of the
bytes (MP3 frames, the WAV header, the Ogg granule) rather than quoted from
the request; the character-cost header reported as what was billed; and the
append-only library of every generation, which /sfx reads back and the help
topic :sfx teaches from.

Left out: the Hermes-only surfaces -- the Desktop sound card, the Desktop
sound library tab and its dashboard backend, and the bundled
elevenlabs-sound-design skill's ffmpeg post-processing script (process,
loopfix, loopcheck, pack) -- because they render in Hermes's own UI or need a
Python runtime, not a cell. Their guidance survives in the tool description
and the :sfx manual topic; the files stay in the sounds folder where any
ffmpeg, editor or game engine can pick them up. The plugin's per-profile
plugin-data directory and plugin.yaml settings schema became the cell's
sound-effects config section.
